  },
  {
    "component": "headline",
    "element": {
      "attributes": {
        "class": "cmc-header-1 xl:mb-5 lg:mb-6 md:mb-6 xs:mb-3 text-center md:max-w-[75%] md:mx-auto",
      },
      "tag": "h1",
    },
    "errors": [],
    "id": undefined,
    "properties": undefined,
    "text": "A 27-year-old woman, 27 million viewers and a thousand-year-old ceremony - this is why Queen Elizabeth II’s Coronation in 1953 was the most famous royal event in history",
    "warnings": [],
  },
  {
    "component": "body",
    "element": {
      "attributes": {
        "class": "cmc-standfirst xl:mb-7 lg:mb-6 md:mb-6 xs:mb-7 text-center md:max-w-[75%] md:mx-auto",
      },
      "tag": "p",
    },
    "errors": [],
    "id": undefined,
    "properties": undefined,
    "text": "<p class="cmc-standfirst xl:mb-7 lg:mb-6 md:mb-6 xs:mb-7 text-center md:max-w-[75%] md:mx-auto">Queen Elizabeth II was crowned in 1953 in a ceremony of pomp and splendour. We tell the story of this momentous day in her life </p>",
    "warnings": [],
  },
  {
    "component": "body",
    "errors": [],
    "text": "<p>Author</p>",
    "warnings": [],
  },
  {
    "component": "body",
    "errors": [],
    "text": "<p>:</p>",
    "warnings": [],
  },
  {
    "component": "body",
    "errors": [],
    "text": "<p>Laura Silverman</p>",
    "warnings": [],
  },
  {
    "component": "body",
    "errors": [],
    "text": "<p>Published Date:</p>",
    "warnings": [],
  },
  {
    "component": "body",
    "errors": [],
    "text": "<p>14 April 2026</p>",
    "warnings": [],
  },
  {
    "component": "body",
    "errors": [],
    "text": "<p>SHARE THIS ARTICLE</p>",
    "warnings": [],
  },
  {
    "alt": "The newly crowned Queen Elizabeth II waves to the crowd from the balcony at Buckingham Palace with her children next to her, she wears a white gown and robe with a crown and the balcony is draped in red curtains",
    "caption": "The newly crowned Queen Elizabeth II waves to the crowd from the balcony at Buckingham Palace | Photo credit: Hulton Archive/Getty",
    "component": "image",
    "credit": "",
    "errors": [],
    "html": "The newly crowned Queen Elizabeth II waves to the crowd from the balcony at Buckingham Palace  |  Photo credit: Hulton Archive/Getty",
    "id": undefined,
    "imageurl": "/_gatsby/file/c533a2857c466fe7bf2dd6eb0d81abaf/4.png",
    "warnings": [],
  },
  {
    "component": "body",
    "element": {
      "attributes": {
        "class": "break-words xs:mb-4",
      },
      "tag": "p",
    },
    "errors": [],
    "id": undefined,
    "properties": undefined,
    "text": "<p class="break-words xs:mb-4">The coronation of Queen Elizabeth II was the most British of days: a day of glory and spectacle, steeped in tradition and uniting the Commonwealth. And it rained. </p>",
    "warnings": [],
  },
  {
    "component": "body",
    "element": {
      "attributes": {
        "class": "break-words xs:mb-4",
      },
      "tag": "p",
    },
    "errors": [],
    "id": undefined,
    "properties": undefined,
    "text": "<p class="break-words xs:mb-4">The day was 2 June 1953, the new <a href="https://www.discoverbritain.com/history/icons/queen-elizabeth-ii-life-reign-timeline/" target="_blank" class="text-primary underline">Queen Elizabeth II</a> had acceded to the throne on 6 February 1952, when her father, King George VI, passed away. The coronation, following a period of mourning and meticulous preparation, marked the occasion. It was to be a day of celebration and solemnity, the centrepiece a ceremony that had remained largely the same for 1,000 years.</p>",
    "warnings": [],
  },
  {
    "component": "body",
    "element": {
      "attributes": {
        "class": "break-words xs:mb-4",
      },
      "tag": "p",
    },
    "errors": [],
    "id": undefined,
    "properties": undefined,
    "text": "<p class="break-words xs:mb-4">There have been 39 coronations held at Westminster Abbey since William the Conqueror’s in 1066, and the bones of the ceremony remain little changed in 1000 years. If you’d like to know more about the ceremony itself, I’d recommend Ian Lloyd’s brilliant <a href="https://amzn.to/4vr0LFa" target="_blank" class="text-primary underline"><i>The Throne: 1,000 Years of British Coronations, </i>which is available on Amazon</a> and details every little bit of history about this ultimate British display of pomp and circumstance. </p>",
    "warnings": [],
  },
  {
    "component": "title",
    "element": {
      "attributes": {
        "class": "cmc-header-2 mb-4",
      },
      "tag": "h2",
    },
    "errors": [],
    "id": undefined,
    "properties": undefined,
    "text": "Queen Elizabeth II’s Coronation",
    "warnings": [],
  },
  {
    "alt": "Queen Elizabeth II awaits the anointing ceremony during her Coronation in June 1953, carried out by the Archbishop of Canterbury. She is in a white dress under a canopy",
    "caption": "The Queen awaits the anointing ceremony, carried out by the Archbishop of Canterbury | Photo credit: Associated Press Photo",
    "component": "image",
    "credit": "",
    "errors": [],
    "html": "The Queen awaits the anointing ceremony, carried out by the Archbishop of Canterbury  |  Photo credit: Associated Press Photo",
    "id": undefined,
    "imageurl": "/_gatsby/file/f22101c03ff7c5f4ad310fe045b379bb/3.png",
    "warnings": [],
  },
  {
    "component": "subtitle",
    "element": {
      "attributes": {
        "class": "cmc-header-3 mb-4",
      },
      "tag": "h3",
    },
    "errors": [],
    "id": undefined,
    "properties": undefined,
    "text": "How old was the queen when she was crowned?",
    "warnings": [],
  },
  {
    "component": "body",
    "element": {
      "attributes": {
        "class": "break-words xs:mb-4",
      },
      "tag": "p",
    },
    "errors": [],
    "id": undefined,
    "properties": undefined,
    "text": "<p class="break-words xs:mb-4">The Queen was 27 at the time. But she had been thinking about the day since she was 11. In May 1937, her father, <a href="https://www.discoverbritain.com/history/icons/king-george-vi/" target="_blank" class="text-primary underline">King George VI</a>, had asked her to record his coronation at <a href="https://www.discoverbritain.com/heritage/heritage-sites/10-facts-westminster-abbey/" target="_blank" class="text-primary underline">Westminster Abbey</a> so that she would appreciate it. “I thought it all very, very wonderful,” she wrote. “The arches and the beams at the top were covered in a sort of haze of wonder as Papa was crowned, or at least I thought so.” Then an admission. The service became “rather boring” towards the end because “it was all prayers”.</p>",
    "warnings": [],
  },
  {
    "component": "body",
    "element": {
      "attributes": {
        "class": "break-words xs:mb-4",
      },
      "tag": "p",
    },
    "errors": [],
    "id": undefined,
    "properties": undefined,
    "text": "<p class="break-words xs:mb-4">The young Queen was unlikely to feel bored when her turn came around, but rather a heady mix of excitement, pride and, perhaps, a little trepidation – not that she showed it. Lady Rosemary Spencer-Churchill, one of her Maids of Honour on the day, recalls her appearing “very confident”. Speaking on BBC Radio 4 in 2013, Lady Rosemary said: “She made us feel very confident too. The minute she arrived, everyone just felt everything was going to be all right.”</p>",
    "warnings": [],
  },
  {
    "component": "subtitle",
    "element": {
      "attributes": {
        "class": "cmc-header-3 mb-4",
      },
      "tag": "h3",
    },
    "errors": [],
    "id": undefined,
    "properties": undefined,
    "text": "Preparations for the day begin",
    "warnings": [],
  },
  {
    "component": "body",
    "element": {
      "attributes": {
        "class": "break-words xs:mb-4",
      },
      "tag": "p",
    },
    "errors": [],
    "id": undefined,
    "properties": undefined,
    "text": "<p class="break-words xs:mb-4">Hundreds of people camped on the pavements the night before in the cold and wet, so that they could catch a glimpse of their new Queen the next day. “The Queen’s subjects remained impressively British in their patient, companionable good nature,” wrote <i>The New York Times</i>. As her subjects waited for the Gold State Coach, wending its way from Buckingham Palace to the Abbey, they sang ‘Singing in the Rain’ and ‘It Ain’t Gonna Rain No More’ with hope and joy at a new Elizabethan era. The crowds would grow on Coronation Day to number three million.</p>",
    "warnings": [],
  },
  {
    "component": "body",
    "element": {
      "attributes": {
        "class": "break-words xs:mb-4",
      },
      "tag": "p",
    },
    "errors": [],
    "id": undefined,
    "properties": undefined,
    "text": "<p class="break-words xs:mb-4">The Abbey, the venue of every coronation since 1066, opened at 6 am to about 8,000 guests. Members of the Royal Family, peers and heads of state from across the world glided in after 8.30 am. Then, at 11.15, came the Queen with a procession of 250 people to verses from <i>Psalm 22</i>. </p>",
    "warnings": [],
  },
  {
    "alt": "The Queen leads the State Procession down the Nave, followed by her Maids of Honour",
    "caption": "The Queen leads the State Procession down the Nave, followed by her Maids of Honour | Photo credit: Mary Evans Picture Library 2015",
    "component": "image",
    "credit": "",
    "errors": [],
    "html": "The Queen leads the State Procession down the Nave, followed by her Maids of Honour  |  Photo credit: Mary Evans Picture Library 2015",
    "id": undefined,
    "imageurl": "/_gatsby/file/effbb31fbdb4d321856eb564db1f145b/5.png",
    "warnings": [],
  },
  {
    "component": "subtitle",
    "element": {
      "attributes": {
        "class": "cmc-header-3 mb-4",
      },
      "tag": "h3",
    },
    "errors": [],
    "id": undefined,
    "properties": undefined,
    "text": "What did Queen Elizabeth II wear for her Coronation?",
    "warnings": [],
  },
  {
    "component": "body",
    "element": {
      "attributes": {
        "class": "break-words xs:mb-4",
      },
      "tag": "p",
    },
    "errors": [],
    "id": undefined,
    "properties": undefined,
    "text": "<p class="break-words xs:mb-4">She wore a gown and a robe of white satin, made by Norman Hartnell, the fashion designer who had also designed her wedding dress. It was embroidered in gold and silver thread with Commonwealth motifs, from the English rose to Canadian maple leaves. The dress is on display as part of the new exhibition<a href="https://www.discoverbritain.com/whats-on/news-events/queen-elizabeth-ii-her-life-in-style/" target="_blank" class="text-primary underline"> Queen Elizabeth II: Her Life in Style this year. </a></p>",
    "warnings": [],
  },
  {
    "component": "body",
    "element": {
      "attributes": {
        "class": "break-words xs:mb-4",
      },
      "tag": "p",
    },
    "errors": [],
    "id": undefined,
    "properties": undefined,
    "text": "<p class="break-words xs:mb-4">As glorious as the Queen looked, the gown and robes were so heavy that they got caught in the thick gold and blue carpet as she walked. “At one moment, I was going against the pile of the carpet, and I couldn’t move at all,” she said in a BBC documentary in 2018. </p>",
    "warnings": [],
  },
  {
    "alt": "Queen Elizabeth II leads the State Procession during her Coronation down the Nave of Westminster, followed by her Maids of Honour, she is wearing her crown and robes of state",
    "caption": "The Queen leads the State Procession down the Nave, followed by her Maids of Honour | Photo credit: Mary Evans Picture Library",
    "component": "image",
    "credit": "",
    "errors": [],
    "html": "The Queen leads the State Procession down the Nave, followed by her Maids of Honour  |  Photo credit: Mary Evans Picture Library",
    "id": undefined,
    "imageurl": "/_gatsby/file/fa02363dc5bc3d52444e60c0ff1146fe/5.png",
    "warnings": [],
  },
  {
    "component": "subtitle",
    "element": {
      "attributes": {
        "class": "cmc-header-3 mb-4",
      },
      "tag": "h3",
    },
    "errors": [],
    "id": undefined,
    "properties": undefined,
    "text": "What was so special about Queen Elizabeth II’s Coronation?",
    "warnings": [],
  },
  {
    "component": "body",
    "element": {
      "attributes": {
        "class": "break-words xs:mb-4",
      },
      "tag": "p",
    },
    "errors": [],
    "id": undefined,
    "properties": undefined,
    "text": "<p class="break-words xs:mb-4">The Coronation was the first to be televised – and the first event many saw on television. About 27 million people tuned in across the UK, with millions more around the world. About 11 million people tuned in on the radio.</p>",
    "warnings": [],
  },
  {
    "component": "subtitle",
    "element": {
      "attributes": {
        "class": "cmc-header-3 mb-4",
      },
      "tag": "h3",
    },
    "errors": [],
    "id": undefined,
    "properties": undefined,
    "text": "What happens at the Coronation of a British monarch?",
    "warnings": [],
  },
  {
    "component": "body",
    "element": {
      "attributes": {
        "class": "break-words xs:mb-4",
      },
      "tag": "p",
    },
    "errors": [],
    "id": undefined,
    "properties": undefined,
    "text": "<p class="break-words xs:mb-4">The three-hour service, conducted by the Archbishop of Canterbury, comprised six parts: the Recognition, the Oath, the Anointing, the Investiture (including the Crowning), the Enthronement and the Homage.</p>",
    "warnings": [],
  },
  {
    "component": "body",
    "element": {
      "attributes": {
        "class": "break-words xs:mb-4",
      },
      "tag": "p",
    },
    "errors": [],
    "id": undefined,
    "properties": undefined,
    "text": "<p class="break-words xs:mb-4">Every object was rich in significance. The Queen was crowned in St Edward’s Chair, made in 1300 for<a href="https://www.discoverbritain.com/history/icons/king-edward-i/" target="_blank" class="text-primary underline"> Edward I </a>and used in <a href="https://www.discoverbritain.com/heritage/royals/coronation-ceremonies/" target="_blank" class="text-primary underline">every coronation since</a>. During the ceremony, the Queen was handed the four symbols of authority: the Orb, the Sceptre, the Rod of Mercy and the Sovereign’s Ring. The Orb, a globe of gold surrounded by a cross with diamonds and emeralds, shone in the light. The Ring, also known as The Wedding Ring of England, glittered with sapphire and rubies. </p>",
    "warnings": [],
  },
  {
    "component": "body",
    "element": {
      "attributes": {
        "class": "break-words xs:mb-4",
      },
      "tag": "p",
    },
    "errors": [],
    "id": undefined,
    "properties": undefined,
    "text": "<p class="break-words xs:mb-4">At the moment of coronation, the Archbishop placed the St Edward’s Crown, one of the most iconic symbols of royalty, on the Queen’s head. Dating back to 1661, it is made of solid gold and encrusted with 440 precious and semi-precious stones. A cry of “God save the Queen” went up, and gun salutes were fired as crowds outside cheered.</p>",
    "warnings": [],
  },
  {
    "alt": "The Archbishop of Canterbury prepares to crown Queen Elizabeth II",
    "caption": "The Archbishop of Canterbury prepares to crown Queen Elizabeth II | Photo credit: Universal History Archive",
    "component": "image",
    "credit": "",
    "errors": [],
    "html": "The Archbishop of Canterbury prepares to crown Queen Elizabeth II  |  Photo credit: Universal History Archive",
    "id": undefined,
    "imageurl": "/_gatsby/file/215d96e9d8f2db2ea5302983a9164bb7/8.png",
    "warnings": [],
  },
  {
    "component": "subtitle",
    "element": {
      "attributes": {
        "class": "cmc-header-3 mb-4",
      },
      "tag": "h3",
    },
    "errors": [],
    "id": undefined,
    "properties": undefined,
    "text": "What happened after Queen Elizabeth II’s Coronation ceremony?",
    "warnings": [],
  },
  {
    "component": "body",
    "element": {
      "attributes": {
        "class": "break-words xs:mb-4",
      },
      "tag": "p",
    },
    "errors": [],
    "id": undefined,
    "properties": undefined,
    "text": "<p class="break-words xs:mb-4">The Queen held a coronation luncheon for 11 in the Abbey annex, where she and the Duke of Edinburgh ate smoked salmon, sausage rolls and chicken sandwiches. A banquet was held for guests, many from abroad, in the Great Hall of Westminster School, next door to the Abbey. The star dish was coronation chicken, a recipe created for the occasion. </p>",
    "warnings": [],
  },
  {
    "component": "body",
    "element": {
      "attributes": {
        "class": "break-words xs:mb-4",
      },
      "tag": "p",
    },
    "errors": [],
    "id": undefined,
    "properties": undefined,
    "text": "<p class="break-words xs:mb-4">For many people outside, the royal procession back from the Abbey to the Palace meant almost as much as the ceremony. At 7.2km, the route was designed to be seen by as many people as possible and took the participants, in their thousands, two hours. The Queen looked resplendent in her Purple Robe of Estate, embroidered with a cipher of the Queen and a border of olive branches and wheat ears, representing peace and plenty.</p>",
    "warnings": [],
  },
  {
    "alt": "Queen Elizabeth II returns to Buckingham Palace after her Coronation at Westminster Abbey she is wearing the imperial state crown",
    "caption": "Queen Elizabeth II returns to Buckingham Palace after her Coronation at Westminster Abbey | Photo credit: The Print Collector / Alamy",
    "component": "image",
    "credit": "",
    "errors": [],
    "html": "Queen Elizabeth II returns to Buckingham Palace after her Coronation at Westminster Abbey  |  Photo credit: The Print Collector  / Alamy",
    "id": undefined,
    "imageurl": "/_gatsby/file/895eb21bd84a1b9d3f67e0035adde812/8.png",
    "warnings": [],
  },
  {
    "component": "subtitle",
    "element": {
      "attributes": {
        "class": "cmc-header-3 mb-4",
      },
      "tag": "h3",
    },
    "errors": [],
    "id": undefined,
    "properties": undefined,
    "text": "The Imperial State Crown",
    "warnings": [],
  },
  {
    "component": "body",
    "element": {
      "attributes": {
        "class": "break-words xs:mb-4",
      },
      "tag": "p",
    },
    "errors": [],
    "id": undefined,
    "properties": undefined,
    "text": "<p class="break-words xs:mb-4">Now she wore the Imperial State Crown, a crown she had also worn at the end of the ceremony. It featured her favourite jewel, the Black Prince’s Ruby, worn by <a href="https://www.discoverbritain.com/history/icons/king-henry-v/" target="_blank" class="text-primary underline">Henry V </a>at the Battle of Agincourt. Both this crown, which the Queen later wore on State Openings of Parliament, and the St Edward’s Crown, were heavy, and despite her grace in the carriage, she endured a feat of strength. As she pointed out in the BBC documentary, “You can’t lean down to read your speech [wearing the crown]... Because if you did, your neck would break, and it would fall off.” To protect her posture, she practised wearing it beforehand. Lady Anne Glenconner, another Maid of Honour, recalled: “She used to wear it when she was writing letters. I think Prince Charles says he remembers going in and seeing her wearing it.”</p>",
    "warnings": [],
  },
  {
    "component": "body",
    "element": {
      "attributes": {
        "class": "break-words xs:mb-4",
      },
      "tag": "p",
    },
    "errors": [],
    "id": undefined,
    "properties": undefined,
    "text": "<p class="break-words xs:mb-4">In 2019, Lady Anne Glenconner published<i> </i><a href="https://amzn.to/4sxHorA" target="_blank" class="text-primary underline"><i>Lady in Waiting</i>, which is available on Amazon</a>. As a Maid of Honour at the Queen’s Coronation and former Lady in Waiting to Princess Margaret, this is a fascinating look into behind the scenes of royal life. </p>",
    "warnings": [],
  },
  {
    "alt": "The Queen with her Maids of Honour at Buckingham Palace",
    "caption": "The Queen with her Maids of Honour at Buckingham Palace | Photo credit: The Print Collector",
    "component": "image",
    "credit": "",
    "errors": [],
    "html": "The Queen with her Maids of Honour at Buckingham Palace  |  Photo credit: The Print Collector",
    "id": undefined,
    "imageurl": "/_gatsby/file/85990a25e5bdaea3c9ffed93e16e86eb/7.png",
    "warnings": [],
  },
  {
    "component": "subtitle",
    "element": {
      "attributes": {
        "class": "cmc-header-3 mb-4",
      },
      "tag": "h3",
    },
    "errors": [],
    "id": undefined,
    "properties": undefined,
    "text": "The Gold State Coach",
    "warnings": [],
  },
  {
    "component": "body",
    "element": {
      "attributes": {
        "class": "break-words xs:mb-4",
      },
      "tag": "p",
    },
    "errors": [],
    "id": undefined,
    "properties": undefined,
    "text": "<p class="break-words xs:mb-4">On the day, the Queen had the added challenge of wearing the crown in a bumpy carriage - the Gold State Coach- a ride she described as “horrible”. She said, “It’s only sprung on leather, not very comfortable.” Just as she found the crown weighty, the horses had similar troubles with the four-ton coach. “We could only go at a walking pace,” said the Queen. “The horses couldn’t possibly go any faster.” </p>",
    "warnings": [],
  },
  {
    "alt": "Queen Elizabeth II waving to the crowds after the coronation ceremony, travelling in the Gold State Coach",
    "caption": "Queen Elizabeth II waving to the crowds after the coronation ceremony, travelling in the Gold State Coach | Photo credit: Bettmann",
    "component": "image",
    "credit": "",
    "errors": [],
    "html": "Queen Elizabeth II waving to the crowds after the coronation ceremony, travelling in the Gold State Coach  |  Photo credit: Bettmann",
    "id": undefined,
    "imageurl": "/_gatsby/file/892e2cf000680942ef500478caacb6d4/6.png",
    "warnings": [],
  },
  {
    "component": "body",
    "element": {
      "attributes": {
        "class": "break-words xs:mb-4",
      },
      "tag": "p",
    },
    "errors": [],
    "id": undefined,
    "properties": undefined,
    "text": "<p class="break-words xs:mb-4">Observers cheered men from the Royal Navy, the Royal Air Force and the Army in the parade, as well as waving to figureheads from across the Commonwealth. But it was Queen Salote of Tonga who captured their hearts, refusing to put the roof up on her carriage, despite the British rain.</p>",
    "warnings": [],
  },
  {
    "component": "subtitle",
    "element": {
      "attributes": {
        "class": "cmc-header-3 mb-4",
      },
      "tag": "h3",
    },
    "errors": [],
    "id": undefined,
    "properties": undefined,
    "text": "The evening celebrations",
    "warnings": [],
  },
  {
    "component": "body",
    "element": {
      "attributes": {
        "class": "break-words xs:mb-4",
      },
      "tag": "p",
    },
    "errors": [],
    "id": undefined,
    "properties": undefined,
    "text": "<p class="break-words xs:mb-4">That evening, for the finale, the new Queen stood on the balcony of Buckingham Palace and turned on a show of lights along the Mall. She wore a silver evening gown, an ermine stole and a diamond diadem, and smiled at the thousands of people looking back at her. Beside her stood her husband, the Duke of Edinburgh, in a dinner jacket. At 10.30 pm, fireworks lit up the sky. “Portraits etched in lines of fire depicted the Queen, the Duke of Edinburgh and their two children, Prince Charles, Duke of Cornwall, and Princess Anne,” recorded <i>The New York Times</i>. “Each display brought a surge of cheering and applause.”</p>",
    "warnings": [],
  },
  {
    "alt": "A black and white photo showing Jubilant crowds congregating on The Mall after the coronation of Queen Elizabeth II in 1953",
    "caption": "Jubilant crowds congregate on The Mall | Photo credit: Fox Photos Ltd.",
    "component": "image",
    "credit": "",
    "errors": [],
    "html": "Jubilant crowds congregate on The Mall  |  Photo credit: Fox Photos Ltd.",
    "id": undefined,
    "imageurl": "/_gatsby/file/25ba945d8ef8cb59c20e97201e12e094/2.png",
    "warnings": [],
  },
  {
    "component": "body",
    "element": {
      "attributes": {
        "class": "break-words xs:mb-4",
      },
      "tag": "p",
    },
    "errors": [],
    "id": undefined,
    "properties": undefined,
    "text": "<p class="break-words xs:mb-4">It was a fitting end to a remarkable day. Recalling her coronation, the Queen later said: “It’s the…beginning of one’s life, really, as a sovereign.” What a life it was.</p>",
    "warnings": [],
  },
  {
    "component": "body",
    "element": {
      "attributes": {
        "class": "break-words xs:mb-4",
      },
      "tag": "p",
    },
    "errors": [],
    "id": undefined,
    "properties": undefined,
    "text": "<p class="break-words xs:mb-4">Many people there on the day have managed to keep and preserve the Queen Elizabeth II Coronation Day book, made to commemorate the day in 1953, and there are copies <a href="https://amzn.to/41uQPwJ" target="_blank" class="text-primary underline">available to buy on Amazon. </a>As we mark 100 years since she was born, there have been several new biographies of Queen Elizabeth II in the last year as well. The world’s most knowledgeable royal biographer, Hugo Vickers, has just released<i> </i><a href="https://amzn.to/47YsB1x" target="_blank" class="text-primary underline"><i>Queen Elizabeth II</i>, which is also available on Amazon</a>, and is a must-read this anniversary year. </p>",
    "warnings": [],
  },
  {
    "component": "body",
    "element": {
      "attributes": {
        "class": "break-words xs:mb-4",
      },
      "tag": "p",
    },
    "errors": [],
    "id": undefined,
    "properties": undefined,
    "text": "<p class="break-words xs:mb-4">This article was first published on <a href="https://www.discoverbritain.com/" target="_blank" class="text-primary underline">DiscoverBritain.com </a></p>",
    "warnings": [],
  },
  {
    "component": "body",
    "errors": [],
    "text": "<p>SHARE THIS ARTICLE</p>",
    "warnings": [],
  },
